The app has completed the build and is running on port 46019. The reason the ports changed is that `just serve --apps=app` rewrote ports.json with newly allocated ports (because the old ports weren't in use). The reason nginx.conf got out of sync again is that the dev servers were launched with `just serve`, which re-resolved the ports.

Current ports.json: app: 46019, cms: 36669, boards: 32853.
But wait — the cms and boards dev servers that are currently running were started on ports 45423 and 43781 (from an earlier ports.json). After that, when I started the app, it re-resolved the ports: since the app's old port 38489 wasn't in use, it got a new one, 46019. And the cms/boards entries in ports.json were… rewritten? Let me check: ports.json now shows cms: 36669, boards: 32853. But the running cms/boards servers are on 45423/43781.

Hmm, `resolveDevServerPorts` preserves saved ports if they're free — but if they're in use, it allocates new ones. Wait, when I launched `just serve --apps=app`, ports.json was {app:38489, cms:45423, boards:43781}. 38489 was closed (the app had been OOM-killed), so app got a new port 46019. But why did cms and boards change to 36669/32853?

Ah, I see — `resolveDevServerPorts` allocates for all DEV_SERVERS (app, cms, boards), not just the selected ones! Looking at the code:

```js
export async function resolveDevServerPorts(stateDir) {
  const stored = readState(join(stateDir, STATE_FILE));
  const taken = new Set();
  const ports = {};
  for (const { name } of DEV_SERVERS) {
    const candidate = stored[name];
    if (Number.isInteger(candidate) && !taken.has(candidate) && (await isPortFree(candidate))) {
      ports[name] = candidate;
    } else {
      ports[name] = await allocateUnused(taken);
    }
    taken.add(ports[name]);
  }
  ...
}
```

It iterates over all DEV_SERVERS. cms: 45423 was in use (the running cms server) → isPortFree(45423) = false → allocated a new port 36669. boards: 43781 was in use → allocated 32853. app: 38489 was free → should have been preserved… no wait, it got 46019.

Hmm, why didn't app keep 38489? 38489 was free (the app server had been killed). However… the previous OOM: was the app's Vite still holding 38489? No, it had been killed. Wait — actually, maybe when I launched the app's serve, 38489 was still in the TIME_WAIT state, or the killed Vite socket was lingering in the listen state… actually, `isPortFree` tests the bind — a port in TIME_WAIT state could cause the bind to fail. That's plausible.

In other words, after the re-render, nginx.conf now points to: app→46019, cms→36669, boards→32853. But the running cms server is on 45423, and boards is on 43781. So the proxy is currently only routing correctly to app!

I keep falling into this port-drift trap. The fundamental issue: `just serve --apps=X` re-resolves the ports for all servers and rewrites ports.json, while the running servers keep their original ports.

I need a single consistent final state. Options:

A) Stop all dev servers, run `just up --detach --no-seed` (render the nginx.conf with the current ports.json), then start all three at once with a single `just serve` (no --apps flag). That way `resolveDevServerPorts` will preserve all three saved ports (all free), and nginx.conf will match. The risk is OOM from 3 simultaneous builds — but all 3 builds are cached now (the app was rebuilt 455 seconds ago, but the cache is warm… hmm, actually each new Vite build takes 2–7 minutes; running 3 simultaneously previously caused OOM).

B) A better approach: start them sequentially with `just serve --apps=X`, but make sure ports.json doesn't drift for the other servers. The drift happens because `resolveDevServerPorts` allocates new ports for the running (in-use) servers. If I stop all dev servers first (making all 3 ports free), then start app alone: app keeps 38489 (free), and cms/boards also keep their saved ports (free). No drift! Then start cms: all 3 ports are free, except app is running → cms keeps its saved port (45423 free), boards keeps its port (43781 free), app's saved port 38489 is in use → app gets a new port → drift again!

Ugh. `resolveDevServerPorts` allocates for all servers on every launch, and running servers get re-allocated.

C) The cleanest way: do it all in a single `just serve` (all 3), and make sure 3 simultaneous builds fit in memory. Previous OOM: containers ~2.3GB + 3×~1.5GB builds = ~6.8GB + overhead → OOM. To make them fit: can I reduce the memory of the running containers? The biggest consumers: keycloak (MaxRAMPercentage=70… but that's 70% of container memory; the container has no mem limit, so it uses host memory), the Spring app (850MB), meilisearch (36MB), localstack, etc.

Alternative: lower parallelism per build? Vite/Angular builds are heavy. The 3 builds each used ~1.5GB. 3×1.5 + 2.3 (containers) = 6.8GB ≈ 7.6GB total → OOM.

D) Or: verify that all 3 can run sequentially while keeping nginx consistent, using the following approach:
   1. Stop all dev servers.
   2. `just up --detach --no-seed` → render nginx.conf with the current ports.json (app:46019, cms:36669, boards:32853).
   3. Start the 3 dev servers sequentially, each with `just serve --apps=X`, and re-render with `just up --detach --no-seed` after each launch? No — re-rendering re-resolves the ports too (it allocates for free servers).

   Actually wait — during `just up`, `resolveDevServerPorts` runs, and it preserves saved ports if they're free. After step 2, ports.json = {46019, 36669, 32853} (all free). Then `just serve --apps=app`: app's saved port 46019 is free → app keeps 46019 and starts there. cms's saved 36669 is free → preserved. boards's 32853 is free → preserved. No drift! The drift only happens when a saved port is in use.

   Next `just serve --apps=cms`: app's saved 46019 is in use (app is running) → app gets a new port → drift in the app entry. Damn.

So the only way to avoid drift with sequential launches is to stop all dev servers before each launch (making all ports free). That's fine — but then the previously launched server goes down. That's no good.

E) What about a single `just serve` with all 3, while making it fit in memory by lowering the memory of the running containers? I could restart the stack with the container memory usage lower, but… that's complex.

F) Or: accept a single `just serve` with all 3 and add swap! I can add a swapfile (I'm root). 4GB of swap would let the OOM pressure survive slow builds. Vite builds swap a bit and become slower, but they complete. This is a clean, non-invasive fix that keeps the documented workflow intact.

Let's add 4GB of swap (fallocate /swapfile, mkswap, swapon), then stop all dev servers, re-render, and run a single `just serve` with all 3. With swap, the 3 builds + containers should survive.

First, let me check the current swap and memory.
